const enBody = `
<h1 id="top">OpenRouter October 2026: API Prices Fell 57-70%, and the Gap Widened</h1>

<p>On November 6, 2026, OpenRouter published its October usage recap. The headline number is that the effective price per token fell 57 to 70 percent since January: closed-weight models went from $1.21 to $0.52 per 1M tokens, and open-weight models went from $0.28 to $0.084. The part that got attention is the second one, because the gap between the two <em>widened</em> from 4.3x to 6.2x at exactly the moment both prices were falling.</p>

<p>Most coverage read that as a paradox. Prices fell, and yet the open-versus-closed gap got worse. The paradox dissolves once you separate three things the headline compresses into one number: the price level, the <em>rate</em> at which it fell, and what "effective" actually means. Open-weight prices did not fall more slowly than closed-weight prices. They fell faster, by 70 percent against 57 percent. A ratio widens when its denominator falls faster, so the widening is arithmetic, not anomaly. Token mix is irrelevant to the ratio itself, because the ratio compares the two price levels directly.</p>

<p>There is a second, less obvious finding that only appears when you pull the underlying data rather than the chart. The <em>reason</em> open-weight models are cheaper is that anyone can serve them, which means dozens of providers compete on the same weights, and the price spread between the cheapest and most expensive provider for a given open-weight model is enormous. Measured across the 25 highest-traffic models in each category, the median provider spread is 4.2x for open-weight models against 3.6x for closed-weight models, and the median open-weight model is served by 13 providers against 8. One model, DeepSeek V4.1 Flash, is served by 39 endpoints priced from $0.006 to $0.45 per 1M input tokens &mdash; a 75x spread on identical weights.</p>

<p>Everything below was pulled from OpenRouter's live APIs and documentation on November 8, 2026. The monthly series was reconstructed from OpenRouter's own published chart, and the reconstruction reproduces their headline endpoints exactly, which is the check that matters before you build an argument on top of a number.</p>

<h2 id="numbers">The five numbers that define October</h2>

<p>The recap contained five points. Taken together they describe a market that grew 25x in a year while getting dramatically cheaper per token, and whose traffic mix changed more than its price list did.</p>

<table>
<thead><tr><th>Metric</th><th>Value</th><th>Comparison point</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Total tokens served in October</td><td>790 trillion</td><td>25x the January figure</td></tr>
<tr><td>Agentic share of all tokens</td><td>76% (~600 trillion tokens)</td><td>8% a year earlier</td></tr>
<tr><td>Median provider cache hit rate on agentic traffic</td><td>76.1% (June) to 90%+ (October)</td><td>Agents resend the same long context every turn</td></tr>
<tr><td>Effective price, closed weights</td><td>$1.21 to $0.52 per 1M tokens</td><td>57% fall since January</td></tr>
<tr><td>Effective price, open weights</td><td>$0.28 to $0.084 per 1M tokens</td><td>70% fall; gap widened 4.3x to 6.2x</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<p>The agentic number deserves more attention than it is getting. An agentic API key is not a chat session; it is a program that resends its entire accumulated context on every single turn. That traffic shape is why caching went from a nice-to-have to a first-order cost lever, and it is also why the effective price per token fell faster than any provider's list price would suggest. If your own workload is agent-shaped, the cache hit rate on your traffic is likely the single highest-leverage number you can measure this quarter.</p>

<h2 id="series">The full monthly series, reconstructed</h2>

<p>OpenRouter published the effective-price series as a chart rather than a table. Digitizing that chart against its own axis gridlines recovers the monthly series, and it reproduces the published endpoints precisely: January closed weights at $1.21, October closed weights at $0.52, January open weights at $0.28, October open weights at $0.084, and the ratios of 4.3x and 6.2x. That reproduction is the validation step. A series that does not land on the vendor's own headline numbers is a series you should not publish.</p>

<table>
<thead><tr><th>Month (2026)</th><th>Closed weights</th><th>Open weights</th><th>Ratio</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>January</td><td>$1.21</td><td>$0.28</td><td>4.3x</td></tr>
<tr><td>February</td><td>$1.14</td><td>$0.25</td><td>4.6x</td></tr>
<tr><td>March</td><td>$0.99</td><td>$0.25</td><td>3.9x</td></tr>
<tr><td>April</td><td>$1.02</td><td>$0.27</td><td>3.8x</td></tr>
<tr><td>May</td><td>$1.15</td><td>$0.23</td><td>4.9x</td></tr>
<tr><td>June</td><td>$1.20</td><td>$0.21</td><td>5.8x</td></tr>
<tr><td>July</td><td>$1.17</td><td>$0.22</td><td>5.4x</td></tr>
<tr><td>August</td><td>$0.63</td><td>$0.16</td><td>3.9x</td></tr>
<tr><td>September</td><td>$0.61</td><td>$0.12</td><td>5.1x</td></tr>
<tr><td>October</td><td>$0.52</td><td>$0.084</td><td>6.2x</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<p>Two features of this series matter more than the endpoints.</p>

<p><strong>First, the collapse is a step, not a slide.</strong> Closed-weight pricing was flat around $1.15 to $1.21 for seven months, from January through July, wobbling inside a 4% band. Then it fell 46% in a single month, July to August, and continued down another 18% over September and October. Open-weight pricing moved differently: a gentle drift down from January to July, then a genuine acceleration from July onward, dropping 27%, 25%, and 30% in three consecutive months.</p>

<p>OpenRouter's own published posts explain the August inflection on the closed side. GPT-5.6 Sol was repriced on August 22, 2026, cutting standard input from $5.00 to $4.00 and output from $30.00 to $20.00, and Sol's own 50% discount started August 17. When the largest closed-weight models repriced at the same moment, a token-weighted index moves like a lever.</p>

<p><strong>Second, the widening happens in the last three months, not across the whole year.</strong> From January to July the ratio actually <em>narrowed</em>, from 4.3x to 5.4x through a dip to 3.8x in April. The gap only opens in August and after, and it opens because open-weight pricing fell faster in that window, not because closed-weight pricing rose. Closed-weight prices also fell in the same period. Both lines go down; the green line goes down faster.</p>

<h2 id="effective">What "effective price" actually means</h2>

<p>The index is not a price list, and conflating the two produces wrong decisions. OpenRouter's own insights post from October 31, 2026 lists the token-weighted <em>list</em> prices over the trailing four weeks as $0.28 input and $1.62 output per 1M tokens for open-weight models, against $1.27 input and $6.36 output for closed-weight models. Those list prices are roughly three times the effective index for open weights and far above it for closed weights.</p>

<p>The gap between list and effective is caching, and it is large. Recomputing from OpenRouter's live rankings endpoint for the week ending November 7, 2026: 198.1 trillion tokens moved across 674 model variants, of which 76.8% was open-weight, 21.6% closed-weight, and 1.7% free-tier. Token-weighting the live list prices gives a blended paid effective rate of $0.535 per 1M tokens. To land on the published October index of $0.52 for closed weights and $0.084 for open weights, you need an implied cache hit rate of roughly 81% and 79% respectively, against a 0.1x cache-read multiplier on list price.</p>

<p>That implied rate is consistent with what OpenRouter reported: the median provider cache hit rate on agentic traffic rose from 76.1% in June to over 90% in October. The index and the cache statistic corroborate each other. The practical consequence is that <strong>the effective price you pay depends far more on your cache hit rate than on which model you pick.</strong> A model with a 10x higher list price but a 95% cache hit rate can be cheaper than a cheap model with a 20% cache hit rate.</p>

<table>
<thead><tr><th>Model (live OpenRouter list price)</th><th>Input</th><th>Output</th><th>Cache read</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>deepseek/deepseek-v4.1-flash</td><td>$0.051</td><td>$0.567</td><td>$0.018</td></tr>
<tr><td>z-ai/glm-5.3</td><td>$0.070</td><td>$1.400</td><td>$0.069</td></tr>
<tr><td>openai/gpt-5.6-luna</td><td>$0.200</td><td>$1.200</td><td>$0.020</td></tr>
<tr><td>xiaomi/mimo-v2.6-pro</td><td>$0.435</td><td>$0.870</td><td>$0.004</td></tr>
<tr><td>google/gemini-3.8-flash</td><td>$0.825</td><td>$4.125</td><td>$0.083</td></tr>
<tr><td>anthropic/claude-sonnet-5.5</td><td>$2.000</td><td>$10.000</td><td>$0.100</td></tr>
<tr><td>anthropic/claude-opus-5.5</td><td>$4.000</td><td>$20.000</td><td>$0.200</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<p>Notice the cache-read column. It is not uniformly 10% of input. DeepSeek V4.1 Flash caches at $0.018, roughly 35% of its input rate, while MiMo V2.6 Pro caches at $0.004, about 1% of its input rate. On a 90%-cached workload, the second model is dramatically cheaper per cached token than the first, despite a list price 8.5x higher. If you are optimizing for cache economics, this column, not the headline input price, is the one to sort on.</p>

<h2 id="why">Why open-weight models are cheaper: provider competition</h2>

<p>OpenRouter's October 31 insights post gives the standard explanation, and it is correct: anyone can serve open weights, so many providers compete to serve the same model, and competition compresses price. The live endpoint data shows how dramatic that compression gets.</p>

<table>
<thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Providers</th><th>Cheapest input</th><th>Most expensive input</th><th>Spread</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>deepseek/deepseek-v4.1-flash</td><td>39</td><td>$0.006</td><td>$0.450</td><td>75x</td></tr>
<tr><td>z-ai/glm-5.3</td><td>47</td><td>$0.070</td><td>$2.800</td><td>40x</td></tr>
<tr><td>qwen/qwen3.8-27b</td><td>24</td><td>$0.034</td><td>$0.990</td><td>29x</td></tr>
<tr><td>moonshotai/kimi-k3</td><td>30</td><td>$0.157</td><td>$4.500</td><td>29x</td></tr>
<tr><td>anthropic/claude-opus-5.5</td><td>11</td><td>$4.00</td><td>$8.00</td><td>2x</td></tr>
<tr><td>anthropic/claude-sonnet-5.5</td><td>10</td><td>$2.00</td><td>$2.20</td><td>1.1x</td></tr>
<tr><td>google/gemini-3.8-flash</td><td>7</td><td>$0.375</td><td>$1.350</td><td>3.6x</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<p>Across the 25 highest-traffic models in each bucket, the median provider spread is 4.2x for open-weight models against 3.6x for closed-weight models, and the median number of competing providers is 13 against 8. The closed-weight spreads are almost entirely the gap between first-party endpoints and resold cloud capacity. Claude Opus 5.5 lists at $4.00 on Anthropic, Amazon Bedrock, Azure and Google Vertex, and one Anthropic route is $8.00 &mdash; a 2x band on a model nobody can compete down, because there is only one set of weights and one vendor selling them.</p>

<p>The open-weight side is different in kind. DeepSeek V4.1 Flash runs at $0.006 per 1M input tokens at the cheapest endpoint and $0.45 at the expensive end, on the same weights, at 39 providers. That 75x band is why the effective price fell. When 39 providers can undercut each other on identical weights, the marginal provider's price is a race toward the marginal cost of serving, and it is much closer to cost than a vendor's list price is.</p>

<p>This is the part that changes procurement decisions. The cheapest provider for an open-weight model is not stable, is not the one you will be routed to by default, and can change week to week. Pinning the provider is a lever that a closed-weight model does not give you at all. If you want a second layer of control on top of provider pinning and cache-aware routing, a gateway like <a href="https://portkey.ai/?ref=apirank" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Portkey</a> will run the same OpenAI-compatible call against several upstreams with your own routing, logging, and budget rules in front of it.</p>

<h2 id="routers">The routers: OpenRouter Auto and Auto Code</h2>

<p>On the same day as the data recap, November 6, OpenRouter launched a rebuilt Auto Router alongside a new Auto Code router. The timing is not a coincidence: the new router exists to capture the fall in effective prices, and its whole design is built around picking the cheapest model that fits each request.</p>

<p>The new Auto Router rates eleven aspects of a request in a single fast decision-model call, including what kind of task it is, whether the task just changed, how hard it is, whether speed matters, whether vision or codebase knowledge is needed, and whether a bigger model or more thinking would serve it better. That rating is then weighed against live prices, cache status, and benchmark performance to choose both the model and the reasoning effort. A second stage, provider routing, then picks which provider serves the chosen model, using the same preferences, uptime, latency, and cache state that apply to a direct request.</p>

<p>Two performance numbers are worth having. The routing step is 3x faster at p90 in OpenRouter's live test, 0.65s against 1.96s, and the new router outperforms the old one by up to 4x depending on the benchmark. Across five coding benchmarks, Auto Code scores in line with Claude Opus 5.5 while costing 74% less per task.</p>

<p>The cost is the interesting part, because there isn't one. You pay the standard rate of whichever model serves the request, and the response's <code>model</code> field tells you which one it was. Sending <code>openrouter/auto</code> is the entire integration.</p>

<pre><code class="language-bash">curl https://openrouter.ai/api/v1/chat/completions \\
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $OPENR...KEY" \\
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \\
  -d '{
    "model": "openrouter/auto-code",
    "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "Add a retry with backoff to fetchUser in api/users.ts"}]
  }'</code></pre>

<p>To see what the router decided and why, enable router metadata. The <code>jev-router</code> stage lists the resolved models, the routed candidates with their reasoning effort and estimated cost, and the reason for the pick.</p>

<pre><code class="language-python">import requests
import json

response = requests.post(
  url="https://openrouter.ai/api/v1/chat/completions",
  headers={
    "Authorization": "Bearer $OPENR..._KEY",
    "Content-Type": "application/json",
    "X-OpenRouter-Metadata": "enabled",
  },
  data=json.dumps({
    "model": "openrouter/auto",
    "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "Summarize this paragraph"}],
    "plugins": [{"id": "auto-router", "cost_tier": "medium"}],
  })
)

data = response.json()
print("Model used:", data["model"])
meta = data.get("openrouter_metadata", {}).get("pipeline", [])
for stage in meta:
    if stage.get("stage") == "jev-router":
        print("Resolved:", stage.get("resolved_models"))
        print("Reason:", stage.get("reason"))
</code></pre>

<p>Four practical settings matter. <code>cost_tier</code> takes <code>low</code>, <code>medium</code>, or <code>high</code>; the default policy deliberately costs more per task than the previous generation so it can consider a wider set of models, and <code>low</code> reproduces the older, cheaper behaviour. <code>allowed_models</code> and <code>excluded_models</code> use pattern syntax and act as hard restrictions, and if they leave no eligible model the request fails with a 404 naming the field to change. Account defaults on the workspace Routing page apply to all three router slugs unless a request overrides them. And <code>session_id</code> is what makes an agent session behave like one conversation rather than a series of unrelated turns.</p>

<p>That last one is not optional in practice. Switching models costs extra while the new model rebuilds the prompt cache, so the router is restrained about switching, favours a reasoning-effort change when that is enough, and in its beta period stayed on the same model for nearly nine of every ten agent turns. Without a <code>session_id</code>, it may switch models between turns, which is fine for exploration and expensive for anything else, because you pay cache-write costs again on every turn the prefix changes.</p>

<h2 id="concentration">The market is also concentrating</h2>

<p>The price story is only half of October. The other half is that volume is concentrating into a very small number of models. From OpenRouter's live weekly rankings for the week ending November 7, 2026, the single most-used model, DeepSeek V4.1 Flash, carried 35.5% of all tokens. The top five models carried 53.8%, and the top twenty carried 81.8%.</p>

<p>That concentration is why the effective index fell so hard. When more than a third of all tokens route to one $0.051-input model, the token-weighted average is that model's price, not the market's. A blend index over a catalog where the top model is a third of volume is not a price-list statistic; it is a volume statistic wearing a price costume.</p>

<p>It also means the index is more fragile than it looks. Doubling the price of the number-one model moves the blended paid effective rate by only about 3%, because it is roughly a third of paid volume. The index is that stable precisely because the leader is cheap. If the leader's price rose tenfold, the blend would shift by roughly 30% while the index would only reflect a tenth of that. Treat the index as a description of what people actually bought, which is the more useful reading anyway.</p>

<h2 id="providers">What this means for what you buy</h2>

<p>Three conclusions follow from the data, and they are different from the usual "prices are down, use a router" framing.</p>

<p><strong>Track your effective price, not the list price.</strong> The list price on a model page is what you pay for an uncached token on a specific provider. The number you care about is your blended rate after caching, and at 90% cache hit rates that number can be a quarter of what the page suggests. If you have not measured your cache hit rate in the last quarter, you are optimizing blind.</p>

<p><strong>Pin the provider for open-weight models.</strong> The spread between the cheapest and most expensive provider on the same open weights is 29x to 75x on the biggest models. Default routing will usually land you somewhere reasonable, but the cheapest endpoint on DeepSeek V4.1 Flash is $0.006 against a $0.45 ceiling, and that is a 75x difference you can have simply by specifying the provider in the request.</p>

<p><strong>Let the router pick the model, but not the provider policy.</strong> The new Auto Router's eleven-factor rating is genuinely better than a hand-rolled heuristic, and it costs nothing extra because you pay the serving model's list rate either way. But you still own the constraints: guardrails, zero data retention, allowed and excluded models, and provider preferences are inputs to the router, not decisions it makes for you.</p>

<p>One more thing for anyone planning capacity. The live catalog on November 8 held 460 entries, 370 unique slugs, 358 paid, and 16 free, and the median paid model price was $0.40 per 1M input tokens and $1.89 per 1M output. Those medians sit well above the effective rates people actually pay, for exactly the reasons above. A model that looks expensive on a median basis may be the cheapest thing available to you, and one that looks cheap may be a poor fit if your cache hit rate is low.</p>

<h2 id="verdict">The verdict</h2>

<p>The 2026 price war did not end. It changed shape. Prices fell 57 to 70 percent in ten months, and they fell because the same weights are sold by dozens of providers who undercut each other, while closed-weight models have one vendor and a band of resellers with no incentive to compete the list price down. The widening gap is arithmetic, not paradox: a faster-falling denominator makes a ratio bigger, and both lines went down.</p>

<p>For an API buyer the actionable items are unglamorous and specific. Measure your cache hit rate before you renegotiate anything, because at current cache economics it dominates model choice. Pin providers on open-weight models, where the spread is 29x to 75x and default routing leaves money on the table. And if your traffic is mixed, the new Auto Router is a genuine improvement with no per-request fee, because it now weighs live prices and cache status directly against an eleven-factor read of each request.</p>

<p>What to watch next: whether the closed-weight side can find a mechanism to compete its own list price down. As of October it has not, and the 6.2x gap is the number to re-check in January.</p>

<p><strong>Sources:</strong> OpenRouter, October 2026 data recap thread on X, published November 6, 2026 (five-point thread plus the effective-price-per-1M-tokens chart); OpenRouter, "Introducing the New Auto and Auto Code Routers" (November 6, 2026); OpenRouter, "Open-Weight vs Closed-Weight Models: What's the Difference?" (October 31, 2026); OpenRouter model catalog API, endpoint listings, and weekly model rankings (verified November 8, 2026); OpenRouter documentation for Auto Router, Auto Code, provider selection, and router metadata (verified November 8, 2026); OpenRouter, "Model Router Benchmarks" (October 2, 2026); OpenAI GPT-5.6 Sol pricing cut, August 22, 2026, as covered by our September 3 analysis.</p>
`;
